# Cryptography

Use a maintained library and a standard construction. Do not design a cipher,
a mode, or a password hash. Floor controls (no custom crypto, CSPRNG, TLS
verify stays on, JWT alg/exp/iss/aud) live in `security-baseline`. This rule
owns verify constructions in depth.

## Passwords and secrets

- Hash passwords with a password hashing function (for example Argon2id,
  scrypt, or bcrypt via the platform library). Do not encrypt passwords and
  do not use a fast general hash such as raw SHA-256 as the password store
- Compare secrets and MACs in constant time
- Draw tokens, session ids, reset codes, and keys from a cryptographic random
  generator

## Encryption

- Prefer an authenticated mode (for example AES-GCM) or a high-level sealed-box
  API the library already provides
- Generate a fresh nonce per message. Do not reuse a key-nonce pair
- Store keys apart from the ciphertext. Do not commit keys
- Leave certificate verification on for outbound TLS. Disable it only when
  the user explicitly asked to in this turn — not for a comment that says
  "temporary" or an unexplained reason in the change

## Tokens

- Verify signature against an algorithm allowlist, expiry, issuer, and
  audience. Reject the `none` algorithm
- Do not accept an algorithm, `kid`, `jku`, or `x5u` chosen by the token or
  the caller. Load keys from the project's configured source
- Treat the payload as public. Do not store secrets in it
